#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8dc2b61-3e0a-5e73-83df-04cc621d5feb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f19dadc-9a47-59fe-8d57-14ca9a0f095a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320372} {:answer/id #uuid "59ca6c49-6b6b-5efa-9d96-1f6004f090cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{-1}{1+C{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320373} {:answer/id #uuid "f814935a-3005-55a2-aacc-7b2a0929f3ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320374} {:answer/id #uuid "58d16a85-9e04-5f05-b7d5-8cf620b3a60b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{-1+Cx}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320375} {:answer/id #uuid "197b642a-007d-568a-ab5d-e44698445089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{-1+C{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f19dadc-9a47-59fe-8d57-14ca9a0f095a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320372} :answer-field/id #uuid "98047d80-b5b5-5e6e-82bb-eb5ebd0f6a0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23f7cb90-301b-5baf-815d-3ddcf267e164" :question/math-academy-id "q-17552" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}={y}^{2}-y\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution* $z={y}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1}\\,\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ -\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-1+z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-1,\\,Q(x)=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-x}$ yields:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}\\cdot (\\frac{dz}{dx}-z) & ={e}^{-x}\\cdot (-1) \\\\ {e}^{-x}\\frac{dz}{dx}-{e}^{-x}z & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dx}(z{e}^{-x}) & =-{e}^{-x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(z{e}^{-x})\\,dx\\, & =-\\int {e}^{-x}\\,dx \\\\ z{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-x}+C \\\\ z & =1+C{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =1+C{e}^{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{x}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
